# Android Layering & Flow

Apply these rules to all Android Kotlin files under `agent_ui/android`.

## Package Structure (Required)

For each feature module, package by layer:

- `com.xyz.<feature>.ui.view`
- `com.xyz.<feature>.ui.viewmodel`
- `com.xyz.<feature>.ui.overlay` (system overlay / floating UI; same dependency rules as `ui.view` for IO bans)
- `com.xyz.<feature>.logic.business`
- `com.xyz.<feature>.logic.data`

Directory layout under `src/main/java` must mirror the declared `package` (each segment is one folder; no `pet` sources living under a legacy `agent/...` path).

## Folder Boundaries (pet demo)

Concrete layout for the pet feature (new work follows the same pattern for other features):

- UI: `**/pet/ui/**` (`ui.view`, `ui.viewmodel`, `ui.overlay`)
- Logic: `**/pet/logic/business/**`, `**/pet/logic/data/**`
- Process hosts (`Service`, etc.): `**/pet/logic/business/platform/**` (thin host only)
- Service start/bind orchestration: `**/pet/logic/business/entry/**` (e.g. platform gateway)

Legacy demo modules (`dynamicui`, `llm`) remain separate until migrated.

## `logic.data` scope (intent)

Prefer **data access implementations** here: network clients, local DB/DAO, file-backed stores, upload/download, CRUD. **Not** system permission UX (e.g. `Settings.canDrawOverlays`), generic process binding, or orchestration—those belong in `ui.viewmodel`, `logic.business`, or `logic.business.platform`. The pet demo may still mix legacy types under `logic.data`; migrate incrementally.

## Android process hosts (Service, etc.)

Types that subclass `android.app.Service` belong in `com.xyz.<feature>.logic.business.platform` (thin host). `startForegroundService` / `bindService` orchestration lives in `com.xyz.<feature>.logic.business.entry` (e.g. `PetPlatformGateway`). The layering checker allows `android.app.*` only under `logic.business.platform`, and allows same-feature `logic.data` to reference that subpackage only—not the rest of `logic.business`.

## API & Flow (Required)

- Prefer stream-based outputs (`Flow`, `StateFlow`, `SharedFlow`; `Flowable` only for explicit Rx integration). Expose state/events as streams, not callback-heavy `onXxx` APIs from logic.
- Build and self-test non-UI APIs first, then integrate into UI.
- UI maps gestures/view events to logic commands and renders from stream state/events.

## Dependency Direction (Required)

Only downward dependencies are allowed:

- `ui.view -> ui.viewmodel, ui.overlay, logic.business`
- `ui.viewmodel -> ui.view, ui.overlay, logic.business`
- `ui.overlay -> ui.viewmodel, ui.view, logic.business, logic.data` (e.g. ASR transport wiring)
- `logic.business -> logic.data`
- `logic.data -> (lower-level libs only)` and same-feature `logic.business.platform` only

Forbidden:

- Any lower-to-upper dependency (for example `logic.* -> ui.*`).
- Cross-feature direct dependency on another feature's `ui.*`.

## UI Framework Isolation (Required)

Android UI framework APIs are only allowed in `ui.*` packages.

`logic.business` and `logic.data` must not import or reference:

- `android.app.Activity`
- `androidx.fragment.app.Fragment`
- `android.view.View`
- `android.widget.*`
- `androidx.compose.*`

Exception: `...logic.business.platform` may use `android.app.*` for Foreground `Service` hosts (validated by `scripts/check_android_layering.py`).

## No Direct IO in View Layer (Required)

Code in `ui.view` must not perform direct IO, including:

- file read/write
- network calls
- direct database access
- manual thread management or blocking calls

`ui.view` only handles rendering and event forwarding. IO must go through `ui.viewmodel -> logic.business -> logic.data`.

## Utils Are Layer-Owned (Required)

`utils` is not an independent architecture layer.

Forbidden:

- generic cross-layer packages such as `com.xyz.utils` or `<feature>.utils` used to bypass layer boundaries.

Allowed:

- `...ui.utils...` (UI-only helpers)
- `...logic.business.utils...` (business-only helpers)
- `...logic.data.utils...` (data-only helpers)
- `com.xyz.common` / `com.xyz.foundation` pure helpers (must not depend on `ui.*` or `logic.*` implementations)

## Testing (Guidance)

For new interaction features, implement API-level self-tests first: state transition sequence, event ordering, and cancel/abort paths (no stray submit events). UI tests are secondary—rendering and gesture mapping only.

## Lint (Guidance)

Prefer static checks (Detekt, ArchUnit, custom lint) for import boundaries. Until available, enforce via review; track adding lint as follow-up.

## Examples

Bad:

- `com.xyz.featureA.logic.data.UserRepo` imports `androidx.compose.runtime.*`
- `com.xyz.featureA.ui.view.HomeScreen` calls `okhttp3.OkHttpClient` directly
- `com.xyz.featureA.utils.JsonExt` is imported by both `ui.*` and `logic.*`

Good:

- `ui.view` delegates user actions to `ui.viewmodel`
- `ui.viewmodel` calls `logic.business` use-cases
- `logic.business` orchestrates and calls `logic.data` repositories
- layer-specific helper code stays in layer-owned `utils`
